The Gaussian construction passes the exact checks, including recovery when a batched gcd returns \(N\). A second concrete deduction uses smooth divisors of \(N^2-1\): they can expose promised common divisors of \(p^2-1\) and \(q^2-1\), whose CRT roots feed a Coppersmith search. The general average-case candidates still need new analytic lemmas. I’m checking their fallback costs without assuming independent failure sets.

